    <section id="about" class="section container two-column">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">About</p>
        <h2>A Curated Seed Dataset Documents AI Slop Patterns in Open-Source Software Triage</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          AI coding assistants and autonomous agents are increasingly contributing to open-source software. However, maintainer-validated examples of plausible-looking but invalid AI-assisted submissions are scattered across platforms, making systematic study difficult. Such “AI slop” can consume scarce review time because maintainers must verify technical claims, reproduce alleged failures, and explain why apparently credible reports are wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
          We present an ongoing empirical study that curates a structured seed dataset of AI slop artifacts from public open-source workflows. A case is included when a stable primary-source artifact is available and maintainer or project evidence shows that the submission is hallucinated, technically invalid, unreproducible, out of scope, misleading, or explicitly identified as AI slop. Each case is coded by project, platform, artifact type, claimed issue or change, AI/slop signals, maintainer or project response, final outcome, and slop category.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our curated seed dataset comprises 11 cases: nine curl vulnerability reports from HackerOne and two RPCS3 pull requests from GitHub. Preliminary coding identifies slop categories including nonexistent functions and fabricated traces, ignored safety checks, dependency-scope confusion, test fixtures misclassified as exposed secrets, documented behavior reframed as a vulnerability, existing functionality falsely presented as new, and nonsensical dependency changes. These cases show how surface-level technical plausibility can conceal fundamental errors and transfer verification work to maintainers. The dataset provides a foundation for expanding case collection, analyzing category distributions, and developing lightweight methods to triage low-quality AI-assisted submissions before extensive human review.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

